{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"rg --files .pose/knowledge | head -30; rg -n 'bounded|cache|runtime' .pose/knowledge; cat .pose/adr/2026-10-14-shared-domain-and-optional-worktree-runtime.md; cat pose-mcp/internal/pose/cache_size.go; cat pose-mcp/internal/pose/test_catalog.go\"","aggregated_output":".pose/knowledge/2026-10-27-handoff-delegation-mcp-round5.md\n.pose/knowledge/2026-11-02-handoff-agency-readiness-outcome-review.md\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-18-handoff-pose-b06-source-coverage-closeout.md\n.pose/knowledge/2026-08-16-decision-log-module-metadata-discovery-invalidates-review-provenance.md\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-20-handoff-pose-b07-implementation.md\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-27-handoff-delegated-rounds-legacy-status.md\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-28-handoff-delegated-conclusions-round4.md\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-14-decision-log-adr-shared-worktree-runtime-review.md\n.pose/knowledge/2026-09-12-note-cli-output-design-taxonomy.md\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-13-decision-log-review-assurance-requires-cryptographic-proof.md\n.pose/knowledge/README.md\n.pose/knowledge/2026-08-13-decision-log-adr-component-aware-review-plans-review.md\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-26-handoff-delegation-observation-round4.md\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-13-decision-log-action-answer-proof-and-historical-time.md\n.pose/knowledge/2026-11-02-handoff-roadmap-v7-independent-outcomes-20261102.md\n.pose/knowledge/2026-08-15-decision-log-github-issue-closing-keyword-format.md\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-14-decision-log-adr-release-temporal-evidence-review.md\n.pose/knowledge/2026-08-13-decision-log-adr-sealed-review-bundles-review.md\n.pose/knowledge/2026-11-04-handoff-pose-b34-implementation.md\n.pose/knowledge/2026-08-14-decision-log-escalation-validate-native.md\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-26-handoff-delegation-routing-round3.md\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-24-decision-log-action-recovery-intent-and-refusal.md\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-09-24-handoff-multirepo-review-continuation.md\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-07-19-handoff-contract-baseline-handoff.md\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-08-13-decision-log-install-one-liner-safe-form.md\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-08-03-decision-log-project-agnostic-assessment-evidence.md\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-08-15-decision-log-self-referential-policy-template-contamination.md\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-08-13-handoff-pr15-component-aware-review-provenance.md\n.pose/knowledge/2026-11-02-handoff-agency-readiness-outcome-review.md:53:These are low residual follow-ups for the bounded technical outcome, not\n.pose/knowledge/2026-11-02-handoff-agency-readiness-outcome-review.md:58:consumer gaps are not demonstrated runtime transport failures. The known\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-07-19-handoff-contract-baseline-handoff.md:209:  runtime (ADR `2026-07-19-mcp-tool-catalog-is-a-release-gated-contract`).\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-08-03-decision-log-project-agnostic-assessment-evidence.md:81:saúde em runtime. Relatórios e documentação devem preservar essa distinção.\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-14-decision-log-adr-shared-worktree-runtime-review.md:3:slug: adr-shared-worktree-runtime-review\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-14-decision-log-adr-shared-worktree-runtime-review.md:16:# decision-log: adr-shared-worktree-runtime-review\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-14-decision-log-adr-shared-worktree-runtime-review.md:20:Review the [domain/runtime proposal](../adr/2026-10-14-shared-domain-and-optional-worktree-runtime.md)\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-14-decision-log-adr-shared-worktree-runtime-review.md:26:Last reviewed: 2026-10-14. Proposal is Proposed; specs draft, no IPC/cache/runtime\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-14-decision-log-adr-shared-worktree-runtime-review.md:34:IPC identity/permissions/platforms, cache accounting/view ownership and measured\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-14-decision-log-adr-shared-worktree-runtime-review.md:36:when the implementation scope is authorized. Lint/merge do not qualify runtime.\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-14-decision-log-adr-shared-worktree-runtime-review.md:41:stdio/UID/cache hits are not authority; watch hints are not current generation.\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-14-decision-log-adr-shared-worktree-runtime-review.md:47:human confirmation or default runtime adoption.\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-14-decision-log-adr-shared-worktree-runtime-review.md:53:- [B33](../specs/2026-10-14-pose-bounded-isolated-graph-caches.md)\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-14-decision-log-adr-shared-worktree-runtime-review.md:54:- [B35](../specs/2026-10-14-pose-optional-worktree-runtime.md)\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-08-15-decision-log-self-referential-policy-template-contamination.md:79:  from the runtime error message and `mcp-enforce/README.md` to the already-\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-08-13-handoff-pr15-component-aware-review-provenance.md:32:  policy-bounded deferral semantics.\n.pose/knowledge/2026-08-13-decision-log-adr-sealed-review-bundles-review.md:42:- Verify policy-bounded targeted criterion reuse and complete final projection.\n.pose/knowledge/2026-11-04-handoff-pose-b34-implementation.md:85:Para Go nesta máquina, usar o cache padrão e `GOTMPDIR`/`TMPDIR` em\n.pose/knowledge/2026-11-04-handoff-pose-b34-implementation.md:86:`/var/tmp/pose-work-go` com a autorização de filesystem aplicável. O cache\n.pose/knowledge/2026-11-04-handoff-pose-b34-implementation.md:87:descartável `/tmp/pose-b34-go-cache` esgotou a quota e foi limpo; não recriá-lo.\n.pose/knowledge/2026-11-04-handoff-pose-b34-implementation.md:119:- [ADR aceito](../adr/2026-11-03-governance-reads-use-bounded-operation-scoped-views.md)\n.pose/knowledge/2026-11-02-handoff-roadmap-v7-independent-outcomes-20261102.md:34:f1 is quoted output drift; runtime qualifies key presence assertions and refuses unsigned MCP answers. No physical hardware or human identity guarantee is inferred. f2 leaves automated outcome evidence dependent on explicit independent judgment. Generic integration assessment has 64 unobserved consumers; two pre-existing invalid spec inputs leave technical-debt scan incomplete. Recurrence reports validate-native flapping but zero flagged failure clusters. These broad observations do not invalidate the freshly exercised roadmap paths.\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-13-decision-log-action-answer-proof-and-historical-time.md:26:A stored label is not a cryptographic result. A signature authenticates signed bytes; it does not make unsigned presence metadata authoritative. Acceptance time and present-day trust are separate inputs. Legacy second-resolution timestamps describe an interval, so historical validation uses its bounded precision rather than pretending a nanosecond clock was retained.\n# ADR: Shared domain and optional worktree runtime\n\n## Status\n\nProposed — 2026-10-14, for B31/B33/B35. No IPC, cache API/schema, daemon lifecycle,\nplatform default or policy is implemented/adopted by this proposal. Reconcile and\nresolve the structural contract before functional start. Preserve accepted ADRs\nfor active-context discovery, scoped Git readers, obligations and the\nMCP-Conductor-Harness validation boundary.\n\n## Context\n\nThe [performance analysis](../reports/2026-10-12-pose-front-08-resident-performance.md)\nseparates computation, transport and freshness costs. CLI-backed Store adapters\nspawn the binary with a fixed deadline; MCP persistence alone does not remove\nrepeated algorithms or graph copies. The [UX analysis](../reports/2026-10-12-pose-front-07-operational-ux.md)\nshows plan/context composition and planning handoff gaps while P15 read parity\nalready works. The [consistency analysis](../reports/2026-10-12-pose-front-09-consistency-recovery.md)\nidentifies nested mutable aliasing and generation/coordination boundaries.\n\nCached graph copies include shallow Nodes.Attributes, misses are not coordinated\nper key, and the single last graph can churn across projects. These are code-founded\nobservations, not measured corruption by a current consumer or a native race.\nA client-owned MCP stdio stream cannot be attached by an unrelated CLI. Existing\nsessions/cursors/registry mutexes do not prove worktree IPC or write serialization.\n\n## Decision\n\nPropose these boundaries for review, without choosing wire/schema details yet:\n\n- Extract one typed domain interface for admitted operations. CLI/MCP adapt syntax\n  and rendering, with common results/coverage/generation/error semantics. Preserve\n  the adapter-not-fork principle; remove the POSE subprocess only where extraction\n  is qualified. Legitimate scoped Git and external Harness execution remain separate.\n- Preserve project selection and actual authority/assurance. Read-only planning or\n  typed CLI handoff does not authorize writes; sharing code, process UID or stdio\n  does not create Execution Identity, signature or human presence. Active-context\n  discovery keeps authorized logical IDs and never reveals unauthorized roots.\n- Keep validation orchestration outside arbitrary tools/call execution. Native\n  structural/read-only evaluations and explicitly admitted operations use the\n  domain; project check execution still requires its existing request/approval/\n  Harness contract. Scheduling, agents, workflow retries and notifications stay\n  outside the POSE core. Bounded admission/in-flight work here is evaluation of\n  requested calls, not a durable workflow queue or an autonomous scheduler.\n- Expose effectively immutable graph views or deep defensive copies at mutable\n  compatibility boundaries. Keys identify worktree, content, parser/engine/schema\n  and complete dependencies/generation; current authorization/verdict is not\n  inferred from a cache hit. Coordinate one production per key, budget retained\n  bytes/pins/waiters per worktree and globally and measure allocations/RAM.\n- Preserve the scoped Git batch-reader decision. Residence does not authorize\n  a shared cat-file stream, persistent/global reader pool or shell expansion.\n  Any future change of its ownership/lifetime needs a separate structural decision.\n- Make runtime optional, identified by user + canonical worktree/Git identity +\n  engine/build/protocol/schema/capabilities. project_id/common Git directory alone\n  cannot distinguish mutable worktrees. A separate protected local IPC endpoint\n  may host this domain; platform transport (Unix socket/named pipe or equivalent),\n  authentication/permissions and compatibility require resolution before code.\n  Never take a client's MCP stdio connection or claim a static doctor sees it.\n- Support explicit attach/start/status/stop/restart and standalone/CI fallback.\n  Restart/update must not inherit stale generation/authority. An ambiguous committed\n  write requires receipt/reconcile before retry through another process; query\n  fallback validates inputs. Watch events are hints; B14/B15/B34 own publication,\n  coordination and coherent read generations, including external edits.\n- Bound requested evaluation concurrency by category/worktree and globally, with\n  CPU quota/affinity, memory/IO/child-process and admission metrics. Protect short\n  queries during heavy work and preserve deterministic output. Fix budgets and\n  benchmark thresholds on comparable corpora before adoption, rather than changing\n  defaults from one historical sample or claiming p95 from three observations.\n\n## Consequences\n\nB31 yields standalone semantic reuse, B33 supplies isolated/bounded graph views,\nand B35 consumes both after B07/B12/B14/B15/B32/B34 prerequisites. API/caches can\nbe qualified without requiring a resident service; B35 stays opt-in after delivery.\nThe exact IPC/protocol, compatible GateResult/envelopes, platform support, cache\naccounting/view ownership and lifecycle/QoS thresholds remain acceptance work.\nNo new root enumeration, command, default, workflow queue or policy is adopted.\nHistorical benchmark numbers and the duration of documentary checks do not prove\nruntime speedup. Consumers retain existing mutable contracts until qualified\nmigration, and the source/effects/assurance remain authoritative.\n\n## Alternatives rejected\n\n- Mirror all .pose into RAM at startup: loads cold history and retains repeated\n  parsing/copying/algorithms without establishing freshness or a memory budget.\n- Reuse the MCP client's stdio as shared IPC: the stream belongs to its initiating\n  client and supplies neither attach compatibility nor authority for another CLI.\n- Fork CLI rules in MCP/runtime: improves transport at the cost of divergent\n  result, validation and authorization semantics.\n- Treat watcher events or mtime/clean as proof: misses external changes and cannot\n  bind a gate/write to complete current inputs.\n- Run arbitrary validation checks in a new in-process MCP helper: bypasses the\n  established execution-identity/approval/Harness boundary.\n- Mandatory autostart and unlimited work/cache: removes CI/standalone fallback,\n  risks memory growth and query starvation without a measured benefit.\n\n## Review triggers\n\nBefore B31/B33/B35 start, settle interface/compatibility, caller mapping, local IPC\nplatform/identity, budgets and benchmark thresholds. Revisit if a scoped reader\nlifetime changes or a new external execution capability is proposed. Track the\nexpiring operational review in knowledge:adr-shared-worktree-runtime-review.\n\n## Amendment: bounded, isolated graph caches (B33)\n\nSpec `pose-bounded-isolated-graph-caches` fixes the cache accounting and view\nownership this ADR left to acceptance work. Rebuildable derived data (the parsed\ndelivery graph, the design-delta report, the test catalog) goes through one cache\nin the domain package; the paths a commit changed are read by the governed capture,\nbounded and not cached:\n\n- **Bytes, not entries.** Each value reports its retained size; the cache evicts\n  the least recently used entries under a global budget and a budget per owner (a\n  worktree root). The parsed-graph budget is 192 MiB global and 96 MiB per\n  worktree. The size is estimated by walking every field of the value (strings,\n  slices, maps, pointers), so no variable-sized field is charged a constant; the\n  estimate is calibrated against the measured heap of the real index (it errs\n  high, about 2.3 times). A value larger than its budget, and a value its producer\n  declares unretainable, is served and neither retained nor copied for retention.\n  An entry a reader pins (a view in progress) stays charged after eviction until the\n  reader lets go, and the production reservation is held until the result is\n  published, so what the cache keeps alive is what it accounts for.\n- **Work in flight.** A production reserves bytes before it runs, globally and per\n  owner, and waits (or leaves with its context) until they fit; the number of\n  concurrent productions, of queued keys (globally and per owner) and of waiters per\n  key is capped, a waiter that leaves gives its place back, and a caller refused for\n  lack of capacity gets `ErrCacheBusy` on every consumer: overload is an error the\n  caller sees, never a production outside the cache. Copies run outside the lock, one of a\n  few copy slots each, so a large copy never delays another key. The clone a caller\n  keeps after it returns is the caller's memory and is outside the budget; a reader\n  that only looks up fields uses a view and keeps nothing.\n  The same holds for the input a caller read before asking and the clone being made\n  for it: they are the caller's memory, and bounding them is a recorded follow-up. A\n  shared result stays charged until its last waiter has copied it. A gate that needs\n  the test catalog treats an overloaded cache as incomplete validation, not as an\n  empty catalog.\n  Every retained entry is also charged its metadata (the entry, its list node, the\n  lookup slots, 256 bytes), its key and its owner, so many small entries cannot exceed the\n  budget while the payload counters stay within it. Publication (the waiters a result has, the removal of the flight, the release of the\n  reservation) is one critical section. The one read whose output is unbounded by\n  nature, the paths of a commit, is bounded as above.\n- **Key.** Parsed graphs are keyed by engine version, schema and the digest of the\n  bytes: identical bytes cannot yield a different graph, so two worktrees holding\n  the same index share one entry and none is served another's graph; the same size\n  or modification time proves nothing. The test catalog is keyed by root and the\n  digest of the validation matrix and expires after 30 seconds, because it reads\n  the working tree; a long-lived server can serve a catalog built before a test\n  changed for up to those 30 seconds when the matrix did not change (the accepted\n  freshness window), never longer. The paths a commit changed are read by the governed capture, which audits every read and so is not served from a cache: the read is bounded instead, in output (4 MiB) and in paths (100,000), and a larger commit answers unknown and marks the governed read unknown.\n  The design-delta report is keyed by root and the digest of its subject and\n  options, metered per worktree, assessed once per key, and retained only when\n  complete: a partial or unknown report comes from a bound or a failed read that a\n  later call must recompute.\n- **Misses.** A miss is produced once per key however many callers ask together;\n  the production runs outside the lock, so one key never delays another. A waiter\n  whose context ends leaves without disturbing the producer or the other waiters.\n  A failure is never remembered (no negative caching): the next call produces\n  again. Producers and waiters are bounded by the callers themselves; no pool or\n  queue is created.\n- **Isolation.** Every value handed out is a deep copy: the retained one is never\n  shared. The design-delta report is copied by reflection, so a field added later is\n  isolated without anyone remembering it; the graph keeps a hand-written copy\n  because it is hot, and a reflection test holds it to the same standard. Read-only consumers that only look up a few fields use a view\n  (`ViewDeliveryIntegrity`), which hands out the retained graph without a copy and\n  forbids modifying or keeping it; the cache never writes a retained value. The\n  aliasing the audit could not reproduce is reproduced and fixed: the attribute\n  maps of the graph's nodes and the refs of the roadmap criteria were shared with\n  the cache.\n- **Not caches of authority.** Nothing here remembers a verdict, a satisfaction or\n  an authority; gates and writes read current inputs. The Git batch reader of the\n  earlier ADR stays scoped: no global pool or shared stream is introduced.\n\nCost, measured on the real 10.9 MB index: a deep copy per hit costs about 7 ms\nand 18 MB of allocation where the shallow copy cost 1 ms and 5.7 MB; the view costs\nabout 39 KB. That is the price of isolation, paid only by consumers that need a\nmutable graph.\n\n## Related specs\n\n- [B31](../specs/2026-10-14-pose-common-domain-and-cli-mcp-handoff.md)\n- [B33](../specs/2026-10-14-pose-bounded-isolated-graph-caches.md)\n- [B35](../specs/2026-10-14-pose-optional-worktree-runtime.md)\npackage pose\n\nimport \"reflect\"\n\n// estimateRetainedBytes estimates the heap a value retains by walking it: the bytes\n// of every string, the backing arrays of slices, the entries of maps and what a\n// pointer reaches, with a fixed cost per header, element and entry. It accounts for\n// every field of the type, present and future, instead of charging a constant for\n// the ones someone remembered (spec pose-bounded-isolated-graph-caches R4). It is an\n// estimate: TestGraphByteEstimateTracksTheMeasuredHeap calibrates it on the real\n// index, and it errs high.\nfunc estimateRetainedBytes(v any) int64 {\n\treturn estimateValue(reflect.ValueOf(v), 0)\n}\n\nconst (\n\testimateHeader   = 16 // a string header, or the share of a slice or map header beyond its elements\n\testimateEntry    = 16 // the bookkeeping a map entry costs beyond its key and value\n\testimateMaxDepth = 32\n)\n\n// estimateValue counts a value once, with its own slot: a string is its header and\n// bytes, a struct is the sum of its fields (so a flat field counts its size), a\n// slice is its header and its elements, a map its header, entries, keys and values.\nfunc estimateValue(v reflect.Value, depth int) int64 {\n\tif !v.IsValid() || depth > estimateMaxDepth {\n\t\treturn 0\n\t}\n\tswitch v.Kind() {\n\tcase reflect.String:\n\t\treturn int64(v.Len()) + estimateHeader\n\tcase reflect.Slice:\n\t\tif v.IsNil() {\n\t\t\treturn 8\n\t\t}\n\t\telem := v.Type().Elem()\n\t\tif isFlat(elem) {\n\t\t\treturn estimateHeader + int64(v.Len())*int64(elem.Size())\n\t\t}\n\t\tn := int64(estimateHeader)\n\t\tfor i := 0; i < v.Len(); i++ {\n\t\t\tn += estimateValue(v.Index(i), depth+1)\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn n\n\tcase reflect.Map:\n\t\tif v.IsNil() {\n\t\t\treturn 8\n\t\t}\n\t\tn := int64(estimateHeader) + 32\n\t\titer := v.MapRange()\n\t\tfor iter.Next() {\n\t\t\tn += estimateEntry + estimateValue(iter.Key(), depth+1) + estimateValue(iter.Value(), depth+1)\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn n\n\tcase reflect.Ptr:\n\t\tif v.IsNil() {\n\t\t\treturn 8\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn 8 + estimateValue(v.Elem(), depth+1)\n\tcase reflect.Interface:\n\t\tif v.IsNil() {\n\t\t\treturn 16\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn 16 + estimateValue(v.Elem(), depth+1)\n\tcase reflect.Struct:\n\t\tvar n int64\n\t\tfor i := 0; i < v.NumField(); i++ {\n\t\t\tn += estimateValue(v.Field(i), depth+1)\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn n\n\tdefault:\n\t\treturn int64(v.Type().Size())\n\t}\n}\n\n// isFlat reports whether a type holds no pointers, strings, slices or maps, so a\n// slice of it is its length times the element size.\nfunc isFlat(t reflect.Type) bool {\n\tswitch t.Kind() {\n\tcase reflect.Bool, reflect.Int, reflect.Int8, reflect.Int16, reflect.Int32, reflect.Int64,\n\t\treflect.Uint, reflect.Uint8, reflect.Uint16, reflect.Uint32, reflect.Uint64, reflect.Float32, reflect.Float64:\n\t\treturn true\n\tcase reflect.Array:\n\t\treturn isFlat(t.Elem())\n\tcase reflect.Struct:\n\t\tfor i := 0; i < t.NumField(); i++ {\n\t\t\tif !isFlat(t.Field(i).Type) {\n\t\t\t\treturn false\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn true\n\t}\n\treturn false\n}\n\n// deepCopyValue copies a value so that the copy shares no slice, map or pointer with\n// the original. It covers every field by construction, so a field added later is\n// isolated without anyone remembering it; the graph keeps a hand-written copy only\n// because it is hot, and a reflection test holds that copy to the same standard.\nfunc deepCopyValue[T any](in T) T {\n\tout := reflect.New(reflect.TypeOf(in)).Elem()\n\tcopyInto(out, reflect.ValueOf(in))\n\treturn out.Interface().(T)\n}\n\nfunc copyInto(dst, src reflect.Value) {\n\tswitch src.Kind() {\n\tcase reflect.Struct:\n\t\tfor i := 0; i < src.NumField(); i++ {\n\t\t\tif dst.Field(i).CanSet() {\n\t\t\t\tcopyInto(dst.Field(i), src.Field(i))\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\tcase reflect.Slice:\n\t\tif src.IsNil() {\n\t\t\treturn\n\t\t}\n\t\ts := reflect.MakeSlice(src.Type(), src.Len(), src.Len())\n\t\tfor i := 0; i < src.Len(); i++ {\n\t\t\tcopyInto(s.Index(i), src.Index(i))\n\t\t}\n\t\tdst.Set(s)\n\tcase reflect.Map:\n\t\tif src.IsNil() {\n\t\t\treturn\n\t\t}\n\t\tm := reflect.MakeMapWithSize(src.Type(), src.Len())\n\t\titer := src.MapRange()\n\t\tfor iter.Next() {\n\t\t\tval := reflect.New(src.Type().Elem()).Elem()\n\t\t\tcopyInto(val, iter.Value())\n\t\t\tm.SetMapIndex(iter.Key(), val)\n\t\t}\n\t\tdst.Set(m)\n\tcase reflect.Ptr:\n\t\tif src.IsNil() {\n\t\t\treturn\n\t\t}\n\t\tp := reflect.New(src.Type().Elem())\n\t\tcopyInto(p.Elem(), src.Elem())\n\t\tdst.Set(p)\n\tcase reflect.Interface:\n\t\tif src.IsNil() {\n\t\t\treturn\n\t\t}\n\t\tinner := reflect.New(src.Elem().Type()).Elem()\n\t\tcopyInto(inner, src.Elem())\n\t\tdst.Set(inner)\n\tdefault:\n\t\tdst.Set(src)\n\t}\n}\npackage pose\n\nimport (\n\t\"bytes\"\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"crypto/sha256\"\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\t\"errors\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"regexp\"\n\t\"sort\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"time\"\n)\n\n// TestCatalog is the set of test names a requirement trace's `test:` refs can\n// resolve to (spec pose-trace-test-refs-resolve).\n//\n// lint-spec used to count trace refs without resolving them, so an invented\n// test name passed the gate: pose-abm-design-basis cited\n// TestABMDesignBasisDigestStable, which never existed. The catalog reads the\n// tracked files, submodules and nested module repositories included, and records what each ecosystem names a\n// test: Go test, benchmark, fuzz and example functions and t.Run subtests;\n// JavaScript and TypeScript it/test/describe titles; Python test_ functions;\n// Rust #[test] functions. A ref that names a tracked file also resolves, and so does a `go test -run`\n// pattern that selects at least one Go test.\ntype TestCatalog struct {\n\tgoTests []string\n\tnames   map[string]bool\n\tslugs   map[string]bool\n\tfiles   map[string]bool\n}\n\nvar (\n\tgoTestFuncRE  = regexp.MustCompile(`(?m)^func (Test\\w*|Benchmark\\w+|Fuzz\\w+|Example\\w*)\\(`)\n\tgoSubtestRE   = regexp.MustCompile(`\\bt\\.Run\\(\\s*\"([^\"]+)\"`)\n\tjsTestTitleRE = regexp.MustCompile(\"\\\\b(?:it|test|describe)(?:\\\\.(?:only|skip|each\\\\([^)]*\\\\)))?\\\\(\\\\s*['\\\"`]([^'\\\"`]+)['\\\"`]\")\n\tpyTestFuncRE  = regexp.MustCompile(`(?m)^\\s*(?:async\\s+)?def (test_\\w+)`)\n\trustTestFnRE  = regexp.MustCompile(`#\\[(?:tokio::)?test\\]\\s*(?:#\\[[^\\]]*\\]\\s*)*(?:pub\\s+)?(?:async\\s+)?fn (\\w+)`)\n\tslugSepRE     = regexp.MustCompile(`[^a-z0-9]+`)\n\n\t// testCatalogCache is bounded in bytes and metered per root, and an entry is\n\t// keyed by the root and the digest of the validation matrix and lives for\n\t// testCatalogTTL: the catalog reads the working tree, so a long-lived server must\n\t// not serve one built before the tests changed (spec\n\t// pose-bounded-isolated-graph-caches R3/R6). A short-lived CLI builds it once.\n\ttestCatalogCache = newBoundedCache(32<<20, 16<<20, testCatalogTTL, func(c *TestCatalog) *TestCatalog { return c })\n)\n\nconst testCatalogTTL = 30 * time.Second\n\nconst testCatalogMaxFileBytes = 2 << 20\n\nfunc testRefSlug(value string) string {\n\treturn strings.Trim(slugSepRE.ReplaceAllString(strings.ToLower(value), \"-\"), \"-\")\n}\n\n// LoadTestCatalogChecked builds the catalog for root once per process. It returns\n// (nil, nil) when Git cannot list the tree, so callers skip resolution rather than\n// report every ref as unresolved, and a non-nil error when the cache is overloaded\n// or the build was interrupted: the validation is then incomplete, and a gate must\n// say so instead of passing every ref. An overloaded cache is retried for a while.\nfunc LoadTestCatalogChecked(root string) (*TestCatalog, error) {\n\tabs, err := filepath.Abs(root)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, nil\n\t}\n\tmatrix, _ := os.ReadFile(filepath.Join(abs, \".pose\", \"indexes\", \"validation-matrix.json\"))\n\tsum := sha256.Sum256(matrix)\n\tkey := fmt.Sprintf(\"%s|%s|%x\", engineVersion, abs, sum[:8])\n\tdeadline := time.Now().Add(testCatalogBusyWait)\n\tvar catalog *TestCatalog\n\tfor {\n\t\tcatalog, err = testCatalogCache.Get(context.Background(), abs, key, 4<<20, func() (*TestCatalog, int64, error) {\n\t\t\tbuilt := buildTestCatalog(abs)\n\t\t\tif built == nil {\n\t\t\t\t// Git could not list the tree: that is not an empty catalog, and it is not remembered.\n\t\t\t\treturn nil, 0, errNoTestCatalog\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\treturn built, built.retainedBytes(), nil\n\t\t})\n\t\tif errors.Is(err, ErrCacheBusy) && time.Now().Before(deadline) {\n\t\t\ttime.Sleep(50 * time.Millisecond)\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tbreak\n\t}\n\tif errors.Is(err, errNoTestCatalog) {\n\t\treturn nil, nil\n\t}\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"pose: the test catalog is unavailable, so test references were not verified: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\treturn catalog, nil\n}\n\n// LoadTestCatalog is LoadTestCatalogChecked for callers that skip resolution when no\n// catalog exists. An overloaded cache is not \"no catalog\": use the checked form\n// wherever a missing catalog would let a ref pass unverified.\nfunc LoadTestCatalog(root string) *TestCatalog {\n\tcatalog, _ := LoadTestCatalogChecked(root)\n\treturn catalog\n}\n\n// testCatalogBusyWait is how long a caller waits for room in an overloaded catalog cache.\nvar testCatalogBusyWait = 30 * time.Second\n\nvar errNoTestCatalog = errors.New(\"pose: the test catalog could not be built\")\n\n// retainedBytes estimates the heap the catalog holds. The catalog is immutable once\n// built, so the cache hands the same value to every caller.\nfunc (c *TestCatalog) retainedBytes() int64 {\n\tn := int64(64)\n\tfor _, t := range c.goTests {\n\t\tn += int64(len(t)) + 16\n\t}\n\tfor _, m := range []map[string]bool{c.names, c.slugs, c.files} {\n\t\tfor k := range m {\n\t\t\tn += int64(len(k)) + 64\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn n\n}\n\nfunc gitTrackedFiles(dir string) ([]string, bool) {\n\tctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), time.Minute)\n\tdefer cancel()\n\tcmd := RawHistoryGitContext(ctx, dir, \"ls-files\", \"-z\", \"--recurse-submodules\")\n\tvar out bytes.Buffer\n\tcmd.Stdout = &out\n\tif err := cmd.Run(); err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, false\n\t}\n\tfiles := []string{}\n\tfor _, rel := range strings.Split(out.String(), \"\\x00\") {\n\t\tif rel != \"\" {\n\t\t\tfiles = append(files, rel)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn files, true\n}\n\n// nestedModuleRepositories returns, relative to root, the top level of every\n// Git repository nested inside root that the validation matrix registers as a\n// module. Such a repository is neither tracked nor a submodule of root (Harne8\n// keeps graphforge this way), yet its tests are the ones root's checks run.\nfunc nestedModuleRepositories(root string) []string {\n\traw, err := os.ReadFile(filepath.Join(root, \".pose\", \"indexes\", \"validation-matrix.json\"))\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil\n\t}\n\tvar matrix struct {\n\t\tModuleOverrides map[string]json.RawMessage `json:\"moduleOverrides\"`\n\t}\n\tif json.Unmarshal(raw, &matrix) != nil {\n\t\treturn nil\n\t}\n\tseen := map[string]bool{}\n\tnested := []string{}\n\tfor module := range matrix.ModuleOverrides {\n\t\tdir := filepath.Join(root, filepath.FromSlash(module))\n\t\tout, err := RawHistoryGit(dir, \"rev-parse\", \"--show-toplevel\").Output()\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\ttop := strings.TrimSpace(string(out))\n\t\trel, err := filepath.Rel(root, top)\n\t\tif err != nil || rel == \".\" || rel == \"..\" || strings.HasPrefix(rel, \"..\"+string(filepath.Separator)) || seen[rel] {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tseen[rel] = true\n\t\tnested = append(nested, filepath.ToSlash(rel))\n\t}\n\tsort.Strings(nested)\n\treturn nested\n}\n\nfunc buildTestCatalog(root string) *TestCatalog {\n\tfiles, ok := gitTrackedFiles(root)\n\tif !ok {\n\t\treturn nil\n\t}\n\tfor _, repo := range nestedModuleRepositories(root) {\n\t\tinner, ok := gitTrackedFiles(filepath.Join(root, filepath.FromSlash(repo)))\n\t\tif !ok {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tfor _, rel := range inner {\n\t\t\tfiles = append(files, repo+\"/\"+rel)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tcatalog := &TestCatalog{names: map[string]bool{}, slugs: map[string]bool{}, files: map[string]bool{}}\n\tfor _, rel := range files {\n\t\tcatalog.files[rel] = true\n\t\tpattern := testFilePattern(rel)\n\t\tif pattern == nil {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tpath := filepath.Join(root, filepath.FromSlash(rel))\n\t\tinfo, err := os.Lstat(path)\n\t\tif err != nil || !info.Mode().IsRegular() || info.Size() > testCatalogMaxFileBytes {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\traw, err := os.ReadFile(path)\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\ttext := string(raw)\n\t\tfor _, re := range pattern {\n\t\t\tfor _, match := range re.FindAllStringSubmatch(text, -1) {\n\t\t\t\t// A name is a substring of the file text: cloned, it does not keep\n\t\t\t\t// the whole file alive in the cache.\n\t\t\t\tname := strings.Clone(match[1])\n\t\t\t\tcatalog.add(name)\n\t\t\t\tif re == goTestFuncRE {\n\t\t\t\t\tcatalog.goTests = append(catalog.goTests, name)\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn catalog\n}\n\n// testFilePattern returns the expressions that name tests in rel, or nil when\n// rel is not a test file.\nfunc testFilePattern(rel string) []*regexp.Regexp {\n\tbase := strings.ToLower(filepath.Base(rel))\n\tswitch {\n\tcase strings.HasSuffix(base, \"_test.go\"):\n\t\treturn []*regexp.Regexp{goTestFuncRE, goSubtestRE}\n\tcase strings.HasSuffix(base, \".rs\"):\n\t\treturn []*regexp.Regexp{rustTestFnRE}\n\tcase strings.HasSuffix(base, \".py\") && (strings.HasPrefix(base, \"test_\") || strings.HasSuffix(base, \"_test.py\")):\n\t\treturn []*regexp.Regexp{pyTestFuncRE}\n\t}\n\tfor _, ext := range []string{\".ts\", \".tsx\", \".js\", \".jsx\", \".mjs\", \".cjs\", \".mts\"} {\n\t\tif !strings.HasSuffix(base, ext) {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tstem := strings.TrimSuffix(base, ext)\n\t\tif strings.HasSuffix(stem, \".test\") || strings.HasSuffix(stem, \".spec\") || strings.Contains(\"/\"+rel, \"/test/\") || strings.Contains(\"/\"+rel, \"/tests/\") || strings.Contains(\"/\"+rel, \"/e2e/\") {\n\t\t\treturn []*regexp.Regexp{jsTestTitleRE}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn nil\n}\n\nfunc (c *TestCatalog) add(name string) {\n\t// Cloned after trimming: a trimmed title is a substring of its padded original.\n\tname = strings.Clone(strings.TrimSpace(name))\n\tif name == \"\" {\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tc.names[name] = true\n\tif slug := testRefSlug(name); slug != \"\" {\n\t\tc.slugs[slug] = true\n\t}\n}\n\n// Resolves reports whether ref, the part after `test:`, names a known test or\n// a tracked file. A Go subtest path resolves through its top-level test, and a\n// JavaScript title may be cited in slug form.\nfunc (c *TestCatalog) Resolves(ref string) bool {\n\tref = strings.TrimRight(strings.TrimSpace(ref), \".`'\\\"\")\n\tif ref == \"\" {\n\t\treturn false\n\t}\n\tif c.names[ref] || c.files[strings.TrimPrefix(ref, \"./\")] {\n\t\treturn true\n\t}\n\tif head, _, ok := strings.Cut(ref, \"/\"); ok && c.names[head] && strings.HasPrefix(head, \"Test\") {\n\t\treturn true\n\t}\n\tif c.slugs[testRefSlug(ref)] {\n\t\treturn true\n\t}\n\t// A trace often cites the -run pattern its check uses. It resolves when\n\t// `go test -run <ref>` would select a test, the way the check itself runs.\n\tif pattern, err := regexp.Compile(strings.SplitN(ref, \"/\", 2)[0]); err == nil {\n\t\tfor _, name := range c.goTests {\n\t\t\tif pattern.MatchString(name) {\n\t\t\t\treturn true\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn false\n}\n\n// UnresolvedTestRefs returns, in trace order, the `test:` refs of trace that\n// resolve to nothing in catalog.\nfunc UnresolvedTestRefs(trace RequirementTrace, catalog *TestCatalog) []string {\n\tif catalog == nil {\n\t\treturn nil\n\t}\n\tunresolved := []string{}\n\tseen := map[string]bool{}\n\tfor _, requirement := range trace.Requirements {\n\t\tif requirement.Entry == nil {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tfor _, ref := range requirement.Entry.Refs {\n\t\t\tname, ok := strings.CutPrefix(ref, \"test:\")\n\t\t\tif !ok || seen[name] || catalog.Resolves(name) {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tseen[name] = true\n\t\t\tunresolved = append(unresolved, requirement.ID+\" test:\"+name)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn unresolved\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
